{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779484533,"nonce":"QV+i82ODKPtiJ8iTFXL1MUYYeIs=","title":"Core graph recurrence contract and cycle initializers","message":"Related: 0258fac, 0866f1d, 75a4665, 8d94424, f451fc4\n\n## Summary\nMake graph recurrence explicit in the core schema/lowering contract instead of relying on invisible cycle detection side effects.\n\n## Problem\n`Graph.step` already treats cycle wires as previous-step values, but Studio/provider schemas do not yet make recurrent edges and cycle initializers first-class. That makes loops such as `network -\u003e mechanics -\u003e feedback -\u003e network` and `cell.hidden -\u003e cell.hidden` ambiguous to users and to future lowering code.\n\n## Decisions\n- Keep the backend split: port wires are real per-step dataflow; `StateIndex` is component-owned persistent state.\n- Do not refactor to a full-State-only backend or force all stateless leaves to become stateful wrappers.\n- A recurrent edge is a one-step delayed port connection. It is valid only with compatible source/target schemas and an initializer or state-derived initial value.\n- Same-step cycles are invalid unless a recurrent cut is explicit.\n- The cycle cut should be stable and representable, not merely whichever DFS back edge the runtime discovers.\n\n## Backend Requirements\n- Add or document a durable wire temporality/kind concept: `instant` vs `recurrent`.\n- Add recurrent-edge initialization metadata and validation errors that identify missing initial values by spec path.\n- Keep `StateIndex` state slots as owned component memory; expose their metadata to Studio/provider schemas where useful.\n- Preserve existing `Graph.step` / `cycle_port_values` behavior as the near-term runtime base.\n- Keep `SimpleStagedNetwork` / current worker lowering as a bridge until arbitrary nested cyclic Studio graphs can be lowered safely.\n\n## Out of Scope\n- Multirate or inner-rollout subgraph execution; track separately.\n- Acausal/fixed-point within-step cycle solving.\n- Mutation of static Equinox module fields during `lax.scan`.\n\n## Done Criteria\n- Core/provider schema can round-trip recurrent edge kind and initializer metadata.\n- Validation distinguishes instant-cycle errors from valid recurrent cycles.\n- Runtime/lowering errors for missing or incompatible recurrent initializers are clear enough for Studio to surface.\n- Existing cyclic graph behavior remains compatible or has a documented migration path.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}